    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137772,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137773,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Implantation cysts arise most commonly in the inferior and superonasal sectors in the midperipheral iris.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137774,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>An implantation cyst appears as a translucent cyst in the iris stroma, containing clear or slightly opaque fluid, which can be associated with secondary glaucoma, corneal oedema, and amblyopia {8352310;9663240;20490997}. Secondary posttraumatic implantation cysts are situated adjacent to the former corneal perforation site.</p>\r\n<p>The clinical differential diagnosis includes uveitis-associated cysts that arise from interepithelial fluid accumulation in the iris and ciliary body {8976700}, cystic benign naevi, cystic uveal melanoma {6888862}, metastases {28882598}, intraocular foreign body {19702716}, and intraocular parasites {28882598}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137775,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital primary cysts are incidental findings in patients, without sex predilection. Posttraumatic secondary cysts occur months or years after trauma or surgery at any age {28882598;31725833}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137776,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Primary cysts are idiopathic. Secondary cysts follow injury or intraocular surgery.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137777,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital cysts originate from entrapped epithelium entering the eye during invagination of the surface ectoderm when the lens vesicle forms in the fourth week of embryogenesis {1739690;4050928}. Secondary posttraumatic and postsurgical cysts originate from surface epithelium that enters the anterior chamber during injury or surgery.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137778,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cysts are whitish with clear fluid in the lumen.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137779,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Primary and secondary cysts are lined by concentric layers of stratified squamous epithelium with or without goblet cells, with or without keratinization {28882598}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:00:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
